-- title:
--   Tangent space bound d(h-d) for first-order deformations
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field of characteristic $p$ for a prime $p$, perfect in the sense of the $p$-power map being bijective, let $d$ be a natural number and let $G_0$ be a $d$-dimensional formal group law over $k$: a $d$-tuple of power series in the $2d$ variables indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\,d \oplus \mathrm{Fin}\,d$ with vanishing constant terms, linear coefficients $\delta_{ij}$ in each of the two blocks of variables, and satisfying associativity; assume $G_0$ is commutative, i.e. interchanging the two blocks of variables fixes each component. Let $h$ be a natural number and assume that $k[[X_1,\dots,X_d]]$ modulo the ideal generated by the components of $G_0$'s $p$-th iterate series (`nthSeries`, defined by iterating $n \mapsto$ substitution of the previous tuple into the first block of $G_0$, starting from $0$) has $k$-dimension $p^h$. Give $k$ the $k[\varepsilon]$-algebra structure in which $\varepsilon$ acts as $0$. For a deformation $D$ of $G_0$ over $k[\varepsilon]$, that is a formal group law $D.F$ over $k[\varepsilon]$ whose coefficientwise reduction is exactly $G_0$, let $\delta(D)$ be the $d$-tuple of power series over $k$ obtained by taking the $\varepsilon$-part of each coefficient of $D.F$. Let $Z$ be the $k$-span of the $\delta(D)$ for commutative $D$, and $\mathrm{Bdry}$ the $k$-span of the $\delta(D)$ for commutative $D$ admitting an isomorphism of deformations (a homomorphism with two-sided inverse whose component series reduce to the variables $X_i$ modulo $\varepsilon$) onto some deformation $D'$ with $\delta(D')=0$. The assertion is that the quotient of $Z$ by the intersection $\mathrm{Bdry}\cap Z$, taken inside $Z$, is a finite $k$-module of $k$-dimension at most $d\,(h-d)$, the subtraction being truncated subtraction of natural numbers.
--
--   This is the upper bound half of the computation of the tangent space $\mathrm{Def}_{G_0}(k[\varepsilon])$ of the deformation functor of a commutative formal group law of dimension $d$ and height $h$, the quotient of symmetric normalised $2$-cocycles by the coboundaries coming from coordinate changes $X \mapsto X + \varepsilon\eta(X)$; for $d=1$ it gives the Lubin–Tate bound $h-1$. It is used in the construction of formal coordinates on Jacobians with good reduction.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_MvFormalGroup_finrank_firstOrderDeformations_le_mul_sub.lean

